// Regression test: a syntax error inside an IMPORTED module must produce a
// clean compile error, not crash the compiler.
//
// Before the fix, ParseFile()'s "skip one token and keep trying" error-
// recovery loop (used when parsing an imported module hits a genuine parse
// error) would limp forward token-by-token through the rest of the already-
// malformed declaration. This eventually read the underlying ifstream past
// EOF repeatedly, which crashed the ENTIRE compiler with an unhandled
// `std::__ios_failure` ("basic_ios::clear: iostream error") instead of
// reporting the real syntax error and stopping cleanly. Confirmed via
// `extern fn broken(ptr: void*): void;` inside an imported module -- `ptr`
// collides with the `ptr` type keyword, which was also a real, separate bug
// in lib/std/mem.tr itself (its extern declarations used `ptr` as a
// parameter name; fixed to use `p` instead -- see mem.tr's own doc
// comment).
//
// Also exercises the companion fix in ParseImport(): a module that IS found
// but fails to parse must not fall through to the OTHER search-path
// fallbacks (which would only ever produce a confusing, unrelated "module
// not found" error stacked on top of the real one).
//
// See broken_extern_param_module.tr for the deliberately broken module this
// imports.

import broken_extern_param_module;

fn main(): int {
    return 0;
}
